---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';
import { loadAllSnapshots, loadManifest } from '@data/loadSnapshot';
import { getPointBudget, minLevelForSpent } from '@domain/talents/rules';

const manifest = loadManifest();
const { ruleset } = manifest;
const snapshots = loadAllSnapshots();

const budgetAt = (level: number) => {
  const b = getPointBudget(level, 'standard', ruleset);
  return typeof b === 'number' ? b : 0;
};
const levelRows = [10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40, 45, 50, 55, 60].map((level) => ({
  level,
  points: budgetAt(level),
}));

const rowCount = 7;
const gateRows = Array.from({ length: rowCount }, (_, i) => {
  const earlier = ruleset.pointsPerRowTier * i;
  return {
    row: i + 1,
    earlier,
    minSpent: earlier + 1,
    minLevel: minLevelForSpent(earlier + 1, ruleset),
  };
});
const capstone = gateRows[rowCount - 1];

const treeRows = snapshots.flatMap((s) =>
  s.trees.map((tree) => {
    const total = s.talents
      .filter((t) => t.treeId === tree.treeId)
      .reduce((n, t) => n + t.maxRank, 0);
    return {
      className: s.classDef.name,
      classId: s.classDef.classId,
      tree: tree.name,
      total,
      diff: ruleset.maxBudget - total,
    };
  }),
);
const fillable = treeRows.filter((r) => r.diff >= 0).length;

// First prerequisite on the Druid page, used as a concrete example.
const druid = snapshots.find((s) => s.classDef.classId === 'druid');
const byId = new Map((druid?.talents ?? []).map((t) => [t.talentId, t]));
const exampleTalent = (druid?.talents ?? [])
  .filter((t) => t.prerequisites.length > 0)
  .sort((a, b) => a.treeId.localeCompare(b.treeId) || a.row - b.row)[0];
const examplePre = exampleTalent
  ? byId.get(exampleTalent.prerequisites[0].talentId)
  : undefined;
---

<BlogPostLayout slug="wow-forever-talent-points-levels-and-row-gates">
  <p>
    Two numbers decide almost everything you can do in the calculator: how
    many points you have, and how many of them already sit in the right
    place. The first depends on level. The second depends on row gates, the
    rule that a talent further down a tree stays locked until enough points
    are spent above it. Both are simple, but the way they interact catches
    people out, especially when they plan a build at level 60 and then wonder
    why it cannot exist at level 45.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything here describes the ruleset this site ships,
    <code>{ruleset.rulesetId}</code>. It is our implementation of the rules,
    built from the data and the game information available to us. If the
    game ever disagrees with it, the game is right and we would like to hear
    about it through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How many points you have at each level</h2>
  <p>
    The first talent point arrives at level {ruleset.pointsStartLevel}, and
    you earn one more with every level after that. In the calculator that
    works out as <em>level minus {ruleset.pointsStartLevel - 1}</em>, with a
    ceiling of {ruleset.maxBudget} points. The level range the calculator
    accepts is {ruleset.minLevel} to {ruleset.maxLevel}, so a level
    {ruleset.maxLevel} character has the full {ruleset.maxBudget}. Below
    level {ruleset.pointsStartLevel} there are no points to spend.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data-table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">Talent points by character level</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Level</th>
          <th scope="col">Points available</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          levelRows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{r.level}</td>
              <td>{r.points}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The standard budget counts only points earned by leveling. The site does
    not model Legacy early-spend effects, which the
    <a href="/#faq">home page FAQ</a> explains. If the game confirms a
    separate budget mode later, it would be added as its own profile rather
    than changing this one.
  </p>

  <h2>Row gates</h2>
  <p>
    Each tree has seven rows. A talent in row 1 is always available. A talent
    in any later row needs a minimum number of points already spent in the
    rows above it, in the same tree. In this ruleset the requirement grows by
    {ruleset.pointsPerRowTier} points per row. Points in other trees do not
    count toward the gate, which is why a build that splits points across two
    trees reaches the lower rows of each more slowly than a build that goes
    deep in one.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data-table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">
        Row requirements and the earliest level for each row
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Row</th>
          <th scope="col">Points needed in earlier rows</th>
          <th scope="col">Points spent after taking one rank here</th>
          <th scope="col">Earliest level</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          gateRows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{r.row}</td>
              <td>{r.earlier}</td>
              <td>{r.minSpent}</td>
              <td>{r.minLevel}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last two columns are plain arithmetic on the ruleset: you need the
    gate points plus the point you spend on the talent itself, and you need a
    level that gives you that many points. They assume the
    earlier points all went into the same tree. The "Needs at least level"
    line in the calculator uses the same formula for whatever you have spent
    so far.
  </p>

  <h3>What the bottom row costs</h3>
  <p>
    Reaching row {capstone.row} means putting {capstone.earlier} points into
    that tree first, so the first rank of a bottom-row talent is point number
    {capstone.minSpent} overall. At level {ruleset.maxLevel} that leaves
    {ruleset.maxBudget - capstone.minSpent} points for everything else,
    whether that is more ranks in the same tree or a second tree. This is the
    real constraint behind most talent decisions in a 51-point system: the
    bottom row is available, but it takes most of the budget to open.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites are a separate rule</h2>
  <p>
    Some talents also need a specific rank of another talent.
    {
      exampleTalent && examplePre ? (
        <>
          {' '}On the <a href="/druid/">Druid page</a>, for instance,{' '}
          {exampleTalent.name} requires {examplePre.name} at rank{' '}
          {exampleTalent.prerequisites[0].requiredRank}.
        </>
      ) : null
    }
    {' '}The snapshot currently records {manifest.coverage.prerequisitesTotal}
    such prerequisites across all classes. A prerequisite works in both
    directions: you cannot add a point until it is met, and you cannot remove
    the prerequisite's last required rank while the dependent talent still
    holds points. Row gates have the same two-way behavior. Removing a point
    from an early row is blocked if it would drop a later talent below its
    gate.
  </p>
  <p>
    The calculator checks the whole build every time, not just the talent you
    clicked, and rejects any change that would leave the build invalid. That
    is why you sometimes have to take points out in a particular order before
    you can move them. The detail panel names the talents that stand in the
    way.
  </p>

  <h2>Can one tree absorb the whole budget?</h2>
  <p>
    Adding up the maximum ranks of every talent in a tree gives a ceiling for
    how many points that tree can hold. Compared with the
    {' '}{ruleset.maxBudget}-point budget, {fillable} of the {treeRows.length}
    trees in the current data hold {ruleset.maxBudget} or fewer, so in
    principle a level {ruleset.maxLevel} character could take every talent in
    those trees and have points to spare. The rest hold more than the budget,
    so something has to be skipped.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data-table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">
        Total ranks per talent tree compared with the point budget
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Class</th>
          <th scope="col">Tree</th>
          <th scope="col">Total ranks</th>
          <th scope="col">Against {ruleset.maxBudget} points</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          treeRows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <a href={`/talents/${r.classId}/`}>{r.className}</a>
              </td>
              <td>{r.tree}</td>
              <td>{r.total}</td>
              <td>
                {r.diff >= 0 ? `${r.diff} spare` : `${-r.diff} over`}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Read this table as a statement about the data, not about good builds.
    Whether filling a tree is sensible is a separate question we do not try
    to answer, and nothing here says one tree is stronger than another. The
    numbers also move if a later game build adds or removes talents, which is
    why the table is generated from the snapshot each time the site is built.
  </p>

  <h2>Planning with these rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Decide the deepest tree first. Its row gates set the earliest level at
      which each of its talents can exist.
    </li>
    <li>
      Treat bottom-row talents as a cost of {capstone.minSpent} points, not
      one.
    </li>
    <li>
      Use the level box to test an intermediate build, for example what a
      level 40 version of your plan looks like, and use
      <a href="/compare/">Compare</a> to see which points you would defer.
    </li>
    <li>
      Remember that Undo and Redo work on the whole build, so exploring a
      risky layout is cheap.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For a hands-on tour of the controls, see the
    <a href="/blog/how-to-use-the-wow-forever-talent-calculator/">calculator guide</a>.
    For what the effect texts mean and how reliable they are, see the post on
    <a href="/blog/where-wow-forever-talent-data-comes-from/">where the data comes from</a>.
  </p>
</BlogPostLayout>
